==English==

===Etymology===
From {{OF.}} ''[[gernier]]'' (variant of [[grenier]]).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈgɑː.nə/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈgɑːɹ.nɚ/}}
* {{audio|en-uk-garner.ogg|Audio (UK)}}
* {{audio|en-us-garner.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|ɑː(r)nə(r)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[granary]]; a store of [[grain]].
#: ''That our garners may be full, affording all manner of store: that our sheep may bring forth thousands and ten thousands in our streets'' (Psalm 144:13, KJV)
# An [[accumulation]] of something.

====Translations====
{{top}}
* French: {{t+|fr|grenier|m}}
{{bottom}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{transitive}} To [[earn]]; to [[acquire]] by virtue of ones [[endeavour]]s.
# {{transitive}} To [[accumulate]] something, especially [[attention]] from the [[media]].
#:''Her new book '''garnered''' high praise from the critics.''
# {{transitive}} To [[reap]] grain, [[gather]] it up, and [[store]] it in a granary.

